 "id": "bundle_ndvi_departure_ait_bouguemez",
 "title": "NDVI departure from the same month, 2019 to 2025 (monthly_departure 1.0.0)",
 "description": "Precomputed result of recipe monthly_departure 1.0.0 on bundle_s2_ndvi_monthly_ait_bouguemez: 58 of 84 periods have a departure. The reference is a short period (2019 to 2025), not a climate normal. A departure describes how this month differs from other years here; it does not say why. The spatial IQR is variability across pixels, not a confidence interval. In winter months the accepted pixels favour the valley floor and south-facing slopes, because snow and shade remove the others; the area value then describes those pixels, not the whole catchment.\n\nRevision 13 (2026-10-27). Among duplicates of a tile and date, an item with no clear pixels over the catchment is now skipped when a twin has some. That clause was added to the duplicate rule after data was first read; the rule fixed before any data was read kept the newest processing baseline only. Revision 12, the first committed, has no version field; it documented the clause but kept the newest item, so it kept an item with 0 clear pixels over the catchment on seven tile-dates: 29RQQ 2019-04-17 (twin had 285 clear pixels), 2019-06-21 (702,669), 2020-11-12 (84), 2021-06-07 (8), 2021-11-19 (8), 2021-12-22 (8), and 29SQR 2020-10-23 (16). In five cases the twin is an older baseline (02.14, 03.00, 03.01), so its clear pixels come from an older scene classification. This revision recomputes 2019-04, 2019-06, 2020-10, 2020-11, 2021-06, 2021-11 and 2021-12 and everything derived from them; no NDVI or departure area median moved at four decimals.",
 "license": "other",
 "version": "13",
